  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nRain continues to be ongoing across portions of the area, mainly \ncentral Wyoming as low pressure continues to move across the area, \nIt is now fairly close to our office just after midnight with \nmoisture wrapping back into the Wind River Basin. Some area had a \nlot of rain yesterday afternoon and evening, with some street \nflooding in Lander. Most of the convective rain is done now, it now \nmainly of the stratiform variety. The main area of rain will \ncontinue move slowly east through the night and into tomorrow with \ngradually drying as the low tracks to the east. We will still have \nthe chance of some flooding today as well, especially later in\nthe day if there is some destabilization of the atmosphere to \nget some thunderstorms to fire. However, the impacted area will\nbe more restricted. Precipitable water values remain over 150 \npercent of normal along and East of the Bighorns. So, we will \nmaintain the flood watch here for the rest of the day. Across \nthe western zones, with the deeper moisture moving away, we will\ncancel the watch with issuance this morning. There will be \nenough moisture for isolated showers and storms to fire back \ninto the Basins East of the Divide and the adjacent mountains, \nbut coverage looks rather sparse (generally less than a 1 out of\n3 chance). Showers should gradually end this evening across the\neastern zones this evening with all areas dry by midnight. Some\nlocations in Johnson and Natrona Counties could pick up 0.75 to\n1.50 inches of additional rain today, especially where storms \npersist. However, not all areas will see heavy rain.\n\nOnce we get past this evening, it looks like we will enter a \nquieter pattern. Monday looks like a nice last day of \nastronomical summer for 95 percent of the area with near normal \ntemperatures and a sunny to partly cloudy sky. There may be just\nenough moisture leftover for an isolated, and this means \nisolated with a capital I, shower or thunderstorm in and near \nthe mountains East of the Divide. The chance is at most 1 out of\n6 though, and nothing strong is expected. The only fly in the \nointment is an upper level low moving northeastward from the \nDesert Southwest that will open into a trough and pass through \nTuesday afternoon through Wednesday morning. The GFS has trended\ntoward the European, so the chance of showers and maybe a few \nthunderstorms looks better. This system is a fast mover so no \nheavy rain or flooding is expected out of it at this time. Any \nprecipitation from this system should clear the area by sunset \non Wednesday. Following that, ridging should build across the \narea and bring dry weather for the rest of the workweek and \npossibly into the start of next weekend. And with generally \nsouthwest flow across the area, temperatures should average \nabove normal, in some cases well above normal. Some of the \nwarmer spots may see highs in the mid or even the upper 80s from\nThursday through Saturday.",
